---
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About the demonstrations | David Blumenau" description="How the portfolio’s product recordings, interactive illustrations and source history were prepared.">
  <main id="main" class="wrap demo-notes">
    <header id="top"><a class="text-link" href="/"><Icon name="arrow-left" /> Back to the work</a><p class="eyebrow">About the material</p><h1>Real products.<br /><em>Room to explore.</em></h1><p>The portfolio combines recorded product workflows with small, self-contained demonstrations. Each serves a different purpose.</p></header>
    <div class="demo-notes-body">
      <section><h2>What the recordings show</h2><p>Product screenshots and videos were captured in October 2026. They show working interfaces, with captions describing the actions. Personal information is hidden where necessary; gallery images are selected safe-for-work artwork. Edited recordings disclose cuts or shortened pauses.</p><p>The native chat recordings use an iPhone Simulator. The Attention recording uses the released Mac app. Pencil Page uses synthetic handwriting with actual model feedback. Conference Notes uses an isolated copy of the real application, fictional attendees and an actual analysis. Its recording reviews that analysis after it completed.</p><a href="/media/media-provenance.json">Read the media provenance records <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a></section>
      <section><h2>What you can try here</h2><p>Forge World is an illustrated map of the projects. Its screens use real captures; its buildings, paths and movement are a designed way to explore the work.</p><p>The Memory Lab uses a reviewed technical sample from the knowledge graph. Its controls change that local sample. Source excerpts preserve the supporting wording, including historical names. The workbench diagrams explain configuration and workflow relationships. The Workbench, Recovery and Gateway labs replay actual controlled executions, with original output and downloadable receipts. Their controls move through saved results.</p><p>The Branch Lab follows a reference through the actual PanelForge interface and fork service in an isolated fixture. Answers are seeded rather than generated. Its comparison recording includes a locally committed, unreleased fix; the capture notes and receipt identify it.</p><p>The Pencil Page Timing Lab selects recorded output from the unmodified Swift scheduler. Drawing changes, check times and request outcomes are fictional inputs. Its separate native recording shows a manual review with real model feedback.</p><p>The native Question Lab uses real Claude inference through an isolated iPhone Simulator and a local API compatibility shim. An automated participant answers its built-in question. This capture includes an unreleased Claude-only correction, and the resulting plan remains unexecuted text. It is separate from Attention’s cross-device question flow.</p><p>The Attention Lab follows an actual Codex terminal task through the shared Attention MCP and a durable native Mac card. An automated participant returns a layout choice, and the same task builds a real downloadable page. Its film separates the live exchange from the later app-update check; only Mac arrival was observed.</p><p>The Listening Lab retains Saturation Listening’s actual waveform and timed-text components. Its original Danish story uses clearly labelled AI-generated Cedar narration, reviewed timing estimates and real waveform data. The recordings use the same fixture; the portrait recording is browser touch emulation.</p><p>The Archive Lab replays an actual isolated Photo Archive index rebuild using three synthetic PNG originals. A separate browser check calculates their hashes and can demonstrate a mismatch by changing a temporary memory copy. The original files remain available unchanged.</p><p>These interactive examples run within the portfolio. They do not connect to private records or operate the underlying services.</p></section>
      <section><h2>What the history establishes</h2><p>The release explorer connects selected decisions with repository tags and source commits. Tags establish checkpoints in the code’s history. A dated capability or historical session does not, by itself, establish what is enabled in production today.</p><p>Case studies separate the source history from the workflow demonstrated in their recordings. Evidence drawers preserve useful detail without putting every technical qualification in the main story.</p><a href="/releases/">Explore the source history <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a></section>
      <section><h2>Files you can take away</h2><p>Studio Lab adapts Kitforge’s actual rendering function so you can create and export a graphic here. Its sample PNG and MP4 files are actual browser downloads. The separate Kitforge sample is an actual export from the complete application. The conference PowerPoint was generated by the application and retains editable objects. Its companion PDF is rendered from that file. Fictional notes remain clearly labelled, and personal template-author metadata has been removed.</p><a href="/sample-files/sample-provenance.json">Read the sample-file provenance <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a></section>
      <section><h2>A few well-made building blocks</h2><p>This site uses Astro, Three.js, React, DM Sans and Fraunces. Fonts are served with the site. Distributed library and font notices are available below.</p><div class="demo-license-links"><a href="/licenses/three.txt">Three.js license</a><a href="/media/listening/react-license.txt">React license</a><a href="/media/listening/react-dom-license.txt">React DOM license</a><a href="/media/listening/scheduler-license.txt">Scheduler license</a><a href="/licenses/dm-sans.txt">DM Sans license</a><a href="/licenses/fraunces.txt">Fraunces license</a></div></section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .demo-notes{padding-block:40px 85px;max-width:1080px}
  header .eyebrow{color:var(--rust);margin:42px 0 20px}
  h1{font-size:clamp(3.3rem,6vw,5.7rem)}
  header>p:last-child{font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--muted);max-width:650px;margin-top:26px}
  .demo-notes-body{margin-top:55px}
  section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1.55fr;gap:14px 65px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-block:30px}
  section h2{font-size:1.85rem;grid-row:1/6;max-width:300px}
  section p,section>a,section>div{grid-column:2;font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--muted)}
  section>a{font-size:.82rem;color:var(--ink);width:fit-content;padding-block:7px}
  .demo-license-links{display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap}
  .demo-license-links a{font-size:.8rem}
  @media(max-width:700px){section{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:16px}section h2{grid-row:auto;max-width:none}section p,section>a,section>div{grid-column:1}}
</style>
